<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amir Khan of Tonk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1768">1768</a>–<a href="/years/1834">1834</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Amir Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> Khan of Tonk was a Pathan soldier of fortune who led a large army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> cavalry in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashwant-rao-holkar">Yashwant Rao Holkar</a> and plundering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> states. In 1817, during the Pindari campaign, he agreed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> to disband his army in return for the state of Tonk, the only Muslim-ruled state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>. His officers founded Jaora.</p></div>
